# Agent discipline

* When the requested outcome is a working capability (a skill, pipeline, or process), the goal is the capability functioning — not the completion of the instance task. Hand-completing the instance task while the capability stays broken ("cowboying a solution") is failure even if the artifact ships; the deliverable is the fixed capability, verified by running the task through it (operator correction, 2026-09-09, corpus-pipeline session: "you got the goal and gradient wrong — the goal is the working skill and working pipeline, not the specific completion of the specific task — it is a meta goal").
* Completion records cite commit hashes, not intentions. A report or memory that says "done, all tests green" before the commit exists has outrun the tree (observed 2026-09-08: the local-swarm-budget teardown was recorded complete in the curator's memory with test counts while the work sat uncommitted). Name the hash, or say the work is uncommitted — a verification claim is only as good as the commit it points at.

# Corpus pipeline traps

* `corpus_tag_chunks` `tag_batch_size` default is 10. The `extract_json_from_response` parser in `hkask-types/src/json_extract.rs` must handle JSON arrays (returns full `[{...}, {...}]`, not just the first object inside the array). If the parser only finds the first `{` instead of the first `[` or `{`, batching silently fails — only 1/N chunks per LLM call get real tags. Test: `extract_json_from_response("[{\"a\":1},{\"b\":2}]")` must return the full array, not `{"a":1}`.
* `corpus_build_prompts` `max_prompts` must be set to `tagged_count × prompts_per_chunk`, NOT a small default. Setting it to 500 or 600 on a 33K-chunk corpus produces a toy dataset. The default of 500 is a footgun — always override it.
* `corpus_query` in-memory index is populated by `corpus_convert`/`corpus_chunk` and `corpus_embed`. On server restart, the index is empty, but `corpus_query` now hydrates it from the DB on the first query (loading all embeddings + `passage_text` via `all_embeddings_with_text`). The `embeddings` table stores `passage_text` alongside the vector since the `passage_text` column migration. DBs created before the migration get `ALTER TABLE embeddings ADD COLUMN passage_text TEXT` on first connection. `corpus_embed` stores the chunk text as `passage_text`; other callers (centroids, curator, swarm) pass `None`.
* `training_assemble_dataset` queries the training server's DB by default, but `corpus_ingest_qa` stores to the corpus DB. Pass `db_path` + `passphrase` to `training_assemble_dataset` to bridge this gap. Without it, the assembler sees zero QA pairs.
* QA quality verification must check SEMANTICS, not just structure. No-duplicates + correct-format + non-empty ≠ quality. Check: (1) boilerplate contamination (repeated phrases across answers), (2) grounding against canonical `grounding-verify` semantics (provenance lattice + substring-match discipline), (3) Bloom distribution evenness, (4) subject diversity (not one imposed frame).

# Rust coding guidelines

* Project scripts are bash — this is a Rust project. Do not introduce Python scripts or a Python dependency for tooling; audit/build scripts live under `kask/scripts/` as bash (operator ruling 2026-09-09: "we are a rust project - not python - we do bash scripts not python scripts. not planning to push the python dependency into the project").
* No `unwrap()` — use `?` to propagate errors. Be careful with indexing.
* Never silently discard errors with `let _ =` on fallible operations. Propagate with `?`, use `.log_err()` for visibility, or handle explicitly.
* No `mod.rs` files — use `src/module.rs` instead.
* New crates: specify `[lib] path = "..."` in `Cargo.toml` (e.g., `gpui.rs`, not `lib.rs`).
* No creative additions unless explicitly requested.
* Build: use `./script/clippy` instead of `cargo clippy`.

# Timers in tests

Use `cx.background_executor().timer(duration).await` in GPUI tests, not `smol::Timer::after(...)` — smol timers aren't tracked by GPUI's scheduler and break `run_until_parked()`.

# GPUI traps

* No `block_on` on the foreground thread — it deadlocks if the future needs the GPUI or tokio executor. Make the method async instead.
* Don't use `Arc::get_mut` on `Thread.messages` — it returns `None` when shared. Clone the inner value, modify, replace the `Arc`.
* Don't busy-spin or use timers to wait for deferred tool results in `end_turn` — let the turn end; results drain on the next iteration.
* `AsyncApp` is not `Send` — background tokio tasks must use `tokio::sync::mpsc` channels with a foreground drainer, not capture `AsyncApp`.
* `cx.background_spawn` panics on tokio-dependent futures (reqwest, tokio::time, etc.) — use `gpui_tokio::Tokio::spawn(&*cx, ...)` instead.
* `tokio::time::timeout` / `tokio::time::sleep` panic inside `cx.spawn` (foreground) tasks — `tokio::time::Sleep` registers with the tokio reactor's timer wheel at poll time, and no tokio reactor is entered on the GPUI foreground thread. Use `cx.background_executor().timer(duration)` + `futures_util::future::select` to race a GPUI-native timer against the future instead. `tokio::select!` + `mpsc::recv()` works because mpsc receivers are executor-agnostic, but `tokio::time::Sleep` is not.
* Center-pane `Item` Toggle must use `Toggle` action (not `ToggleFocus`). After `add_item_to_active_pane`, explicitly call `page.focus_handle(cx).focus(window, cx)` if focus delegates to a child entity.

# Pull request hygiene

- Imperative PR title, no conventional commit prefixes, no trailing punctuation.
- `Release Notes:` section as final section, blank line after heading, one bullet (`- Added/Fixed/Improved/N/A`).
- Commit messages are plain text at the `git commit` boundary — never include the markdown fences used to present the message in chat. A bare ``` first or last line leaks the chat presentation into the commit subject (97 commits on main, 2026-07→09); the `kask/githooks/commit-msg` hook (wired via `git config core.hooksPath kask/githooks`) rejects fenced messages.

# Rules hygiene

* `.rules` are traps to avoid, not maps to follow. No architectural descriptions.
* New rules must be: non-obvious, repeatedly encountered, and specific enough to act on.
* Single-crate rules go in that crate's `.rules`, not the repo root.
* Don't edit `.rules` inline during feature work — propose additions in PR descriptions.

# Agent loop traps

* **Tool-call arguments can be cut mid-JSON with no MaxTokens warning.** Via OpenRouter, GLM finishes tool-call streams with `finish_reason="stop"` instead of `"tool_calls"` (the D36 drain in `crates/language_models/src/provider/open_router.rs`), so an output-length cut mid-arguments never surfaces as MaxTokens — the turn ends "cleanly" and the signature is `Error parsing input JSON: EOF while parsing a string at line 1 column N` (observed live: a `render_template` context cut at 9707 chars, 2026-09-03; the 2026-09-01 memory_insert thread-kills match the same signature). Keep large tool payloads split — render templates in batches, pass file references instead of inline content; this applies to `spawn_agent` delegation messages too (observed 2026-09-08: a read-only review delegation requesting ~2.6M tokens failed before execution, "endpoint maximum context exceeded" — pass file paths, not contents). Distinct disease: a call that executes with a *subset* of parameters (corpus_chunk carrying 3 of 8) is model parameter-dropping under huge prompts, not transport truncation.
* **Multi-edit `edit_file` calls apply sequentially and can fail half-applied.** Each edit is applied in order; when edit N matches multiple locations the call fails — but edits 1..N-1 are already on disk (observed 4×, 2026-09-05..08, plus thread evidence). After any multi-edit failure, re-read the file to establish which edits landed before retrying; scope every `old_text` with enough surrounding context to be unique, and count occurrences of a repeated pattern first.
* **Composition breaks at the seam, not the stage.** When chaining tool calls or pipeline stages, verify stage N's output shape is what stage N+1 consumes *before* running the chain — wrong-shape reads fail silently (serde ignores a field placed at the wrong nesting level) or partially (the corpus JSON parser took the first `{` and dropped the rest of the array; a model under a huge prompt supplied only 3 of 8 parameters; a single-prompt watermark marked unseen turns as covered). Round-trip or count-reconciliation checks catch seam breaks; eyeballing the shapes does not.

# zed-kask integration traps

## Divergence surface

`DIVERGENCE.md` defines what's ours (`kask/`) vs upstream Zed. Don't "fix" upstream files speculatively — push fixes into `kask/` behind a D-seam. If an upstream edit is unavoidable, add a D-seam entry + test in the same PR. Every `// zed-kask:` comment disabling upstream behavior needs a test pinning the disabled behavior.

A zed-side edit carries its DIVERGENCE.md seam update in the same pass — the seam record is part of the edit, not a follow-up. An unrecorded zed-side change is indistinguishable from upstream drift at the next rebase, and no later audit can tell deliberate divergence from an agent that skipped the bookkeeping.

`cargo machete` in `script/clippy` is scoped to `kask/` on purpose — don't widen it to the repo root. Root-scope machete flags ~30 deps in upstream `crates/` (mostly `tracing`, `component`) as false positives from macro/feature-activated usage; those belong to upstream CI, not us. Silencing them with `[package.metadata.cargo-machete] ignored` entries means editing ~30 upstream Cargo.tomls (rebase friction) and masks genuinely-dead deps behind "intentionally unused" assertions. A machete finding in `kask/` is always actionable — verify zero `use <dep>` hits in `src/` and `tests/`, then remove the dep line.

## Failure signals

* Every `set_*` hook wired conditionally must `log::warn!` on failure — operators can't distinguish "not configured" from "configured but broken" without it. `OnceLock` hooks must warn on the `Err` branch of `set`; `Mutex` hooks are re-settable and don't need it.
* Numeric env vars that fail to parse must `log::warn!` naming the malformed value, not silently fall back.
* Opt-in `HKASK_USE_*` features that fail must log the failure classification (HTTP status, error variant), not collapse to `None` via `.ok()?`.
* `unwrap_or(0)` on regulation-loop sense inputs is a broken feedback loop — a DB outage returns 0, which the loop reads as "no deviation." Propagate the error or emit `tracing::warn!`.

## Skill system

* `lisp_eval` tool — registered in `add_default_tools`. Do NOT register it again in `register_session` — causes "Duplicate tool name" panic.
* `render_template` tool — registered in `add_default_tools` (alongside `lisp_eval`). Path traversal protection via `canonicalize` + `starts_with` — do not bypass this check. Template base path wired via `agent::set_template_base_path()` (OnceLock) in `main.rs` at startup. If the base path is not wired, the tool returns an error — it does not silently render from the wrong path.
* Convention priors from `.rules` must be verified against the codebase before use — rules can be stale.

## Agent typing system (hkask-mcp-swarm)

* The `PortRegistry` in `hkask-mcp-swarm/src/port_registry.rs` has landed. Port labels (`accepts`/`produces` on `LocalAgentCard`) are type references, not free strings. Every label must resolve to a registered type. A new label requires a registry entry in the same PR.
* The `classify_request` heuristic in `local_runtime.rs` was deleted — it had no correct setting (widen it and it swallows structured ports, narrow it and it misses real declarations). `check_bind` returns `Some(true)` only for `accepts: ["text"]` (universal accept) and `None` for everything else. The typing layer at admission (`validate_typing` + `PortRegistry`) is the gate; runtime classification of free-text requests is the typing layer's unfinished transition. Do not re-introduce a runtime classification heuristic.
* The minimal JSON Schema validator in `hkask-mcp-swarm/src/schema_validate.rs` supports 7 keywords (type, properties, required, items, enum, const, oneOf). Unsupported keywords are NOT a pass — `ValidationStatus::UnsupportedSchema`. Runs AFTER typing, BEFORE the payload is consumed.

## Settings and constants

* Kask settings defaults live in `Default` impls only — not in `#[serde(default)]`, `From<Content>` literals, or `mcp_env()` comparison literals.
* Model-name constants must reference `hkask_inference::model_constants::DEFAULT_*_MODEL`, not re-declare literals across crates.
* MCP server credentials/config are scoped per-server via allowlists. New servers use `Some(&[])` for both, never `None`. Allowlists must align with actual env-var reads.
* After `INFERENCE_SOCKET_PATH` is set, call `sync_kask_mcp_servers` again so servers restart with updated env.
* **Passphrase rotation ordering invariant.** There is ONE passphrase (`HKASK_DB_PASSPHRASE`, default `"allostery"` on first run) for every SQLCipher DB (curator, swarm memory, kata-kanban, research, training). `rotate_all_kask_db_passphrases` MUST complete (all DBs, with rollback) before the new passphrase is written to the keychain — a partial rotation plus a keychain write leaves DBs unopenable. The settings UI (`security.rs`) follows this ordering: rotation → `Ok(())` → write keychain → `nudge_mcp_servers`. A failed rotation logs `warn` and does NOT save the new passphrase. There is no separate swarm-memory passphrase — the swarm server resolves `HKASK_DB_PASSPHRASE` via the canonical helper.

## MCP server patterns

* Tool responses are `{"content": <value>}` envelopes — use `unwrap_tool_envelope`, don't re-implement.
* Error classification must be per-variant, not blanket `McpToolError::internal`.
* Tool inputs accepting arbitrary JSON must use `AnyJsonValue`, not `serde_json::Value` (schemars renders `Value` as bare `true`, which breaks strict-schema providers).
* Live-mutation probe suites must run with `--test-threads=1` and keep probes self-contained.
* Missing credentials must surface as `McpToolError::permission_denied` with the env var named in the message, not as `unavailable`, `invalid_argument`, `failed_precondition`, or a silent fallback. Canonical pattern: read credential from `ctx.credentials.get("ENV_VAR")` → if `None`, return a typed error → map to `permission_denied` at the tool boundary. See `hkask-mcp-swarm/src/abw_client.rs:require_auth` for the reference implementation. A missing credential is an authorization failure, not a transient unavailability or a bad argument. Silent fallbacks (empty results, in-memory DB, skipped env injection) are broken feedback loops — the operator cannot distinguish "not configured" from "no results" or "provider down."
* The canonical `HKASK_DB_PASSPHRASE` resolution helper is `hkask_mcp_server::server::resolve_db_passphrase(&ctx.credentials)` — a 2-tier chain (ctx.credentials → resolve_credential which does env → hkask-keystore keychain). All MCP servers that consume `HKASK_DB_PASSPHRASE` must use this helper, not inline re-implementations. See `hkask-mcp-kata-kanban` for the reference pattern.
* There is NO `service=hkask` keychain fallback for API keys — `resolve_credential` reads API keys from env vars only (injected by `build_mcp_server_env` which reads zed's keychain). The legacy `service=hkask` namespace is fully removed and purged at startup (`hkask-keystore/src/keychain.rs`) — do not write anything to it; entries silently vanish. The single internal key `hkask_db_passphrase` lives in the `kask://credentials/` namespace; there is one passphrase for all SQLCipher DBs and no separate swarm-memory passphrase. Do NOT add `*_enabled` settings toggles — the key's presence in the keychain IS the toggle. A missing key is `permission_denied`, not a silent fallback.
* `write_credential` / `delete_credential` for a credential URL that feeds MCP server env must call `nudge_mcp_servers(cx)` after the keychain write — without it the running server keeps the old key until next launch. The nudge fires `SettingsStore::notify_observers` → `sync_kask_mcp_runtime_servers` → `build_mcp_server_env` (re-reads keychain) → restart.

## Dead code patterns

* Trait-with-one-impl is speculative generality — grep for `self.<field>` before merging; if only constructor matches, it's unwired. This generalizes to: convention helpers with only test callers are dead code, and folded-service modules with no production callers outside the subtree are dead surface.
* Advertised invariants (doc comments claiming security gates, audit surfaces, migrations) must point to the enforcement line. If no enforcement point exists, the doc must say "not yet enforced." Validation gates must return `Undetermined`/`Skipped` when the check wasn't performed, not `Ready` with empty findings.
* **Stale comments are active misinformation.** When code behavior changes, every comment describing the old behavior must be updated in the same PR. Agents read comments as ground truth and will follow stale comments over user instructions — a comment saying "waits for user login" when the code no longer waits causes agents to reproduce the old gating pattern. Grep for comments referencing the changed behavior (e.g. `post-login`, `pre-login`, `user resolves`, `logging mode`) and update or delete them. A stale comment is worse than no comment — no comment lets the agent read the code; a stale comment makes the agent trust the comment over the code.
* Cargo.toml deps outlive their consumers — when deleting a source file, grep for `use <dep>` in remaining `src/` and `tests/`; zero hits = remove the dep.
* Deletions must clean up what they orphan — no orphan piles, no compatibility states (operator ruling, 2026-09-09). When a change deletes rows/entries/entities, the coupled references die in the same change: deleting h_mems removes their embeddings and memory_links (`MemoryStore::delete_h_mem`/`prune_candidates`/`delete_h_mems_by_entity_prefix` self-clean; `delete_orphaned_embeddings` is the periodic backstop), deleting a source file sweeps its dep, deleting a capability sweeps its settings knobs and doc sections. A delete that leaves coupled rows behind shifts cleanup onto every future reader.
* MCP servers are leaf crates (zero external dependents). `pub mod` → `pub(crate) mod` tightening in leaf crates is churn — no external crate can observe the difference. Focus dead-code hunts on truly unused items (zero references anywhere, including `tests/`), not visibility tightening. Check `tests/` before declaring an item dead — test seams (`#[doc(hidden)] pub fn from_stores`) are alive even with zero `src/` callers.
* Every removal ends with a full-repo symbol sweep AND a full build before any green claim: `grep -rn` every removed identifier over code and docs (the 2026-09-08 teardown left `RegulationCycleEntry` in three doc files and a settings reference because the sweep was .rs-only), and `cargo check -p zed` for API changes (a scoped two-crate check missed `kanban_panel`'s removed-field construction — the build-break class the concurrent-edit trap names). An `#[allow(dead_code)]` added or left behind trips `kask/scripts/check-no-new-dead-code-allows.sh`; the sweep and the build are the agent's, the gate is the backstop.

## Other traps

* Built-in file tools (`write_file`, `edit_file`, `create_directory`) are sandboxed to the project root. Writes under `~/Documents/zk-data` (the companies-mcp, corpus-mcp, media-mcp, portfolio-mcp data dirs) fail with a misleading "parent directory doesn't exist" even when the directory exists. Write those files via `terminal` heredoc (split long content into multiple `>>` appends) or via the owning MCP server's tools (observed live 2026-09-03, Polaris PIF report save).
* `LanguageModelInferencePort` honors `model_override` via registry resolution — silently dropping it caused inference calls to use the wrong model.
* `.desktop` files must never declare `text/plain`, `application/x-zerosize`, `x-scheme-handler/zed`, or `Keywords=zed` — run `check-desktop-no-collision.sh` before committing.
* Stale diagnostics after bulk edits: the crate's lib root is authoritative, not individual-file diagnostics. Don't retry stale diagnostics or fix phantom errors.
* LLM-improves-against-LLM-scored-target loops can game the scorer. Three mitigations required: ground in real sources, preserve load-bearing properties, validate against external ground truth.
* `grep -q` exits at the FIRST match — piping a large body into it under `set -o pipefail` SIGPIPEs the writer, and `if !` flips a successful match into a false flag (observed 2026-09-10: the prescreen's overlap check carried this latently since birth; reproduced at ~35% per check on 41KB bodies, `PIPESTATUS: 141 0`). Feed grep from a file or herestring, never `printf | grep -q` on large strings.
* Never read a file in the same parallel tool batch that edits it — the read races the write and returns pre-edit content (observed 2× 2026-09-10: a prescreen run raced its own edit mid-apply; a goals.rs read raced the edit). Sequence reads and edits of the same file across batches.
* In a shared tree, check the staged index (`git diff --cached --stat`) before any `git commit` — the operator's staged work rode into the agent's deletion commit under an unrelated message (observed 2026-09-10: commit 3cf1f7e4a5 carried ~10 files of staged operator work).
# Test protocol traps

* Cross-seam tests assert category invariants derived from the tree at test time, never hardcoded file-name lists. A test on one side of a seam (agent_skills seeding, agent's render_template) must walk the registry and assert every file of the handled category is covered — a name list breaks on every content rename and silently misses every addition (observed 2026-09-09: the seed test pinned eqm-catalog.yaml/capability-ontology.yaml/swarm-patterns.yaml by name inside agent_skills, so every new reference YAML required editing the crate; operator ruling — replaced with the filesystem-derived `collect_seedable_files` walk).

* MCP tool tests must construct the server with the capability under test (embedding store, inference port). A test over a capability-stripped constructor (`try_new_without_embeddings`, no inference) can only pin degradation, never function — the curator "semantic" tools shipped broken for months because every test used the stripped constructor.
* Degradation tests must assert the degradation is SURFACED (a note/status/mode field naming the reason), never that an empty result equals success. A test asserting `count == 0` on the degraded path enforces the broken behavior as spec.
* Recall-path tests must cover the entity_ref JOIN, not just the write: an embedding stored under an entity with no h_mem is an orphan the KNN path silently drops. Test the recall round-trip for every writer variant (curator turns AND non-curator turns).
* When a correctness fix breaks a test, read the test's intent before its assertion — the setup may pin the wrong premise while the purpose is right (observed 2026-09-10: `durable_protection_carries_no_degradation_label` asserted the right behavior over a conflated driver default; the intent stayed, the setup got honest). Fix the setup, keep the intent.

# Concurrent-edit trap

* A build-breaking half-edit left in the tree (e.g. an API change without its call sites) blocks every other agent's validation and is a release blocker, not work-in-progress. If you cannot finish a refactor in one pass, do not leave the tree broken — gate it, branch it, or revert it.
